    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "2 Solution of a relaxed problem"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 25,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.768, 215.95, 477.484, 321.554 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "One possible candidate for F is two straight line segments, from (0,0) to (1,a) and from (1,a) to (0,1), with ρ just sufficient to compensate d, such that e(0,0,F(t),t) = 0,∀t ∈ [0,1]. However, for a < 21, this will not be a geodesic: instead, there will be straight lines from (0,0) to a point beyond (1,a) whose Dirichlet length is greater than the length of F to that point. As a result, the geodesic will skip the steeper line segment to (1,a) and take such a straight line as a shortcut to F, and then follow part of the other line segment to (0,1). Similarly, if a > 12, it will follow part of the line segment from (0,0) to (1,a) and then shortcut the steep segment to proceed directly to (0,1)."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 28,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.768, 156.174, 477.48, 213.957 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "This suggests that we should try to solve for a metric where the segment from (0,0) to (1,a) is not skipped by such a shortcut. In other words, we should ask what is the lowest rate metric for which there is a geodesic candidate F, so that the distance from (0,0) along F to any point on F is greater than the distance of a shortcut from (0,0) to that point."
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 29,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 133.768, 119.562, 477.482, 154.181 ],
    "font" : "CMR10",
    "font size" : 9.963,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
